  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given an integer AAA. Let's define an infinite sequence S(A)=A%P,A2%P,A3%P,…S(A) = A\\%P, A^2\\%P, A^3\\%P, \\ldotsS(A)=A%P,A2%P,A3%P,…, where P=108+7P = 10^8+7P=108+7 is a prime and %\\%% is the modulo operator.\n\nLet's also define a decreasing sum D(S)D(S)D(S) as the sum of all elements of a sequence SSS which are strictly smaller than all preceding elements of SSS. When SSS is a sequence of non-negative integers, the number of such elements is clearly finite.\n\nFind D(S(A))D(S(A))D(S(A)).",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤5001 \\le T \\le 5001≤T≤500",
      "2≤A&lt;P2 \\le A &lt; P2≤A&lt;P"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "The first line of the input contains a single integer TTT denoting the number of test cases. The description of TTT test cases follows.\nThe first and only line of each test case contains a single integer AAA.",
    "outputFormat": "For each test case, print a single line containing one integer D(S(A))D(S(A))D(S(A)).",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n2\n100000006\n5",
        "output": "3\n100000007\n11",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nExample case 1: The sequence S(2)S(2)S(2) is 2,4,8,…,1,2,…2, 4, 8, \\ldots, 1, 2, \\ldots2,4,8,…,1,2,… and thus, D(S(2))=2+1=3D(S(2)) = 2 + 1 = 3D(S(2))=2+1=3.\nExample case 2: The sequence is 108+6,1,108+6,1,…10^8+6, 1, 10^8+6, 1, \\ldots108+6,1,108+6,1,… and thus, D(S(108+6))=108+6+1=108+7D(S(10^8+6)) = 10^8+6 + 1 = 10^8+7D(S(108+6))=108+6+1=108+7."
      }
    ]
  },
